---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const breathCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: '2600 + (tv/2)*(1 - cos(2*pi*f*x/60))', label: 'lung volume', color: '#1d4ed8', width: 2.4 },
  ],
  params: [
    { name: 'f', label: 'breathing rate', min: 8, max: 40, step: 1, value: 15, unit: 'breaths/min' },
    { name: 'tv', label: 'tidal volume', min: 400, max: 2500, step: 50, value: 500, unit: 'mL' },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 12, label: 'time (s)' },
  y: { min: 1000, max: 4200, label: 'lung volume (mL)' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 340,
};

const labCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'f', label: 'breathing rate', min: 8, max: 40, step: 1, value: 15, unit: 'breaths/min' },
    { name: 'tv', label: 'tidal volume', min: 400, max: 2500, step: 50, value: 500, unit: 'mL' },
    { name: 'sa', label: 'alveolar surface area', min: 40, max: 100, step: 5, value: 70, unit: 'm²' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'minute ventilation', expr: 'f*tv/1000', unit: 'L/min' },
    { label: 'alveolar ventilation (150 mL dead space)', expr: '(tv - 150)*f/1000', unit: 'L/min' },
    { label: 'relative O₂ diffusion capacity (∝ area)', expr: 'sa/70', unit: '' },
  ],
};

const ventCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: 'diaphragm flattens, external intercostals lift ribs', note: 'Inhaling: the diaphragm contracts and flattens; the external intercostal muscles pull the ribs up and out.' },
    { math: 'thorax volume ↑ → pressure ↓', note: 'Boyle\'s law in a chest: more volume means lower pressure inside the lungs — now below atmospheric.' },
    { math: 'air flows IN down the pressure gradient', note: 'Air is pushed in by the atmosphere itself; the lungs do not suck.' },
    { math: 'muscles relax (quiet) — internal intercostals contract (forced)', note: 'Exhaling at rest is elastic recoil: the diaphragm relaxes and domes up. During exercise, the internal intercostals pull ribs down to force air out faster.' },
    { math: 'volume ↓ → pressure ↑ → air flows OUT', note: 'Smaller chest, higher pressure than the atmosphere, air leaves. Ventilation is just bulk flow down pressure differences — no biology required until the alveoli.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/biology/intermediate/gas-exchange-in-humans">
  <h2>The idea: a surface built for exchange</h2>
  <p>
    Gas exchange needs a surface, and evolution has packed one into your chest. The syllabus
    (unit 11.1, Core) lists four features of a good exchange surface, and the alveoli —
    roughly 300 million microscopic air sacs — have all of them: a <strong>large surface
    area</strong> (about <strong>70 m²</strong> in an adult, half a tennis court folded into
    your ribcage), a <strong>thin surface</strong> (each alveolus and its capillary share a
    wall one cell thick, so diffusion distances are under a micrometre), a <strong>good blood
    supply</strong> to keep the concentration gradients steep, and <strong>good
    ventilation</strong> to keep refreshing the air side. Oxygen diffuses in, carbon dioxide
    diffuses out, and nothing has to chase anything.
  </p>

  <h2>Ventilation: how air is moved</h2>
  <p>
    The airways the syllabus wants you to identify (Core point 2) — trachea, bronchi,
    bronchioles, alveoli with their capillary nets — form a branching tree from one tube to
    300 million terminals. Only at the alveolar endings does exchange happen; everything above
    them is plumbing. That plumbing is actively serviced: the Supplement expects you to know
    that rings of <strong>cartilage</strong> (point 7) hold the trachea open like a hosepipe,
    that <strong>goblet cells</strong> secrete the mucus which traps pathogens and particles
    before they reach the alveoli, and that <strong>ciliated cells</strong> sweep that
    mucus-laden debris upward to the throat to be swallowed (point 11).
  </p>
  <p>
    Moving the air itself is a volume pump, and the mechanism (Supplement points 6–8) is pure
    physics once you see it:
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="inhale, exhale — five steps from muscle contraction to bulk flow" widget="equationStep" config={ventCfg} />

  <p>
    The result is a rising and falling lung volume. At rest you breathe about
    <strong>15 times a minute</strong> with a <strong>tidal volume near 500 mL</strong>; in
    hard exercise both climb (rates above 30 per minute, tidal volumes of 2–3 litres). Drag
    the sliders to trace what your lungs are doing right now versus mid-sprint:
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="lung volume against time — resting breathing vs exercise; the wiggle height is tidal volume, its frequency is breathing rate" widget="plotter" config={breathCfg} />

  <h2>Inspired vs expired air</h2>
  <p>
    The air you breathe out is not the air you breathed in. The classic test is
    <strong>limewater</strong>, which turns milky in carbon dioxide (Core practical, point 3):
    breathe through a straw into a flask of limewater and it clouds within seconds, while the
    control flask left open to room air barely changes. A cold mirror held to the lips fogs
    with expired breath because expired air is saturated with water vapour. The standard
    composition figures (Core point 4):
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Oxygen:</strong> about <strong>21%</strong> inspired → about <strong>16%</strong> expired — only about a quarter of the oxygen is extracted per breath.</li>
    <li><strong>Carbon dioxide:</strong> about <strong>0.04%</strong> inspired → about <strong>4%</strong> expired — a hundredfold rise.</li>
    <li><strong>Water vapour:</strong> variable in inspired air → expired air is <strong>saturated</strong> (alveoli are wet).</li>
    <li><strong>Nitrogen:</strong> about 78% in both — it takes no part in respiration.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Not all inhaled air reaches the exchange surface: about <strong>150 mL</strong> sits in the
    trachea and bronchi as "dead space". That is why alveolar ventilation, not minute
    ventilation, is the number that matters:
  </p>

  <Widget no="3" caption="how much air actually reaches the alveoli — plus how surface area scales diffusion" widget="sliderLab" config={labCfg} />

  <h2>Exercise, CO₂ and the brain's CO₂ meter</h2>
  <p>
    Working muscles respire faster, and the waste — carbon dioxide — is what drives you to
    breathe harder. The Supplement chain (point 10) is worth memorising in order:
    <strong>respiration rises → blood CO₂ concentration rises → the brain detects the increase
    → it signals the breathing muscles → rate and depth both increase</strong> → the extra
    ventilation flushes the CO₂ out. Notice it is a negative-feedback loop with the brain as
    the sensor and the diaphragm and intercostals as the effectors — you never decide to
    breathe harder during a sprint; the CO₂ decides for you.
  </p>
  <p>
    And while you are at it, note the defences on the way in (Supplement point 11):
    <strong>goblet cells</strong> secrete mucus that traps pathogens and particles,
    <strong>ciliated cells</strong> sweep the mucus upward to the throat, and rings of
    <strong>cartilage</strong> (point 7) keep the trachea open like a hosepipe.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Cambridge IGCSE Biology 0610 syllabus (2026–2028), unit 11; OpenStax Anatomy and Physiology, ch. 22 (The Respiratory System); West, Respiratory Physiology: The Essentials, 11th ed.</p>
</TopicPage>
